sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch07-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Where the tension at cell–cell contacts exceeds that at cell–medium surfaces, the external angle between neighbouring cells is less than 120°, and if the cell–cell tension equals or exceeds the sum of the other two the cells touch only at a point, which explains rounded-off blastomeres separated by spaces.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Strong cell–cell tension relative to cell–medium tension makes cells round off";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "T_{cc} > T_{cm} \\Rightarrow external angle < 120^\\circ; T_{cc} \\ge T_{cm} + T'_{cm} \\Rightarrow contact only at a point";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates”; topic “Unequal tensions and rounding of blastomeres”. Scope: blastomeres of segmenting eggs, pollen tetrads and filamentous algae in contact with a surrounding fluid"
}
